   "w": 560,
   "maxH": 900,
   "page": "everyone",
   "fill": "blue",
   "size": "m",
   "text": "What changes for end users\n\n• Home leads with your environments as power strips: one light per VM, green = running. Click a card to open it.\n• The environment page reads top to bottom: name, lights by start order, what you can do, then the VMs. Cost and settings come after.\n• View-only people see a note and a way to ask for operator access, never buttons that fail.\n• Start shows what it will cost and when it stops before you confirm.\n• Pages you can't use say so instead of going blank.\n• Sentence case, plain words: 'Lock environment', 'Start order 1 · after Database'.\n\nEvery screen fits one 1440 × 900 window: no horizontal scrollbar, and no vertical one either. Long lists show a page of rows with page controls; extra detail sits in tabs or a side column.\n\nAt scale: 15 environments fit one page (38 px rows). A 54-VM environment shows every light in one strip, then one group at a time with Running / Stopped / Problems filters and 10 VMs per page. Over 24 VMs, a strip inside a list becomes a bar."
  },
  "t-envadmin": {
   "x": 0,
   "y": -320,
   "text": "Environment admin — Manage section",
   "kind": "title1",
   "maxW": 4480,
   "page": "envadmin"
  },
  "s-envadmin": {
   "x": -700,
   "y": 0,
   "w": 560,
   "maxH": 900,
   "page": "envadmin",
   "fill": "blue",
   "size": "m",
   "text": "What changes for environment admins\n\n• Access requests is a clean review queue: who, what, how long, current access, Approve / Deny that never overlap (today they overlap the 'Current access' column).\n• Automation rules shows each schedule as a week strip, so 'Mon–Fri 08:00–20:00' is visible, not read.\n• Every switch has a name (today ~45 are announced as just 'on').\n• Search filters as you type: the extra Search and Refresh buttons go.\n• Help '?' links go to the right topic (My Environments pointed to Locks).\n• Grant access (new screen): several people × several environments or groups in one go, with a preview of what is new, extended, upgraded or skipped. Today: one person, one environment at a time.\n\nEvery screen fits one 1440 × 900 window: no horizontal scrollbar, and no vertical one either. Long lists show a page of rows with page controls; extra detail sits in tabs or a side column."
  },
  "t-admin": {
   "x": 0,
   "y": -320,
   "text": "Administrator — Cost and Administration",
   "kind": "title1",
   "maxW": 4480,
   "page": "admin"
  },
  "s-admin": {
   "x": -700,
   "y": 0,
   "w": 560,
   "maxH": 900,
   "page": "admin",
   "fill": "blue",
   "size": "m",
   "text": "What changes for administrators\n\n• Home is a to-do list first (requests, failed runs, drift, budgets), then the fleet and spend. Today it has nothing to click but Refresh.\n• Cost management: one Actions menu and one Export menu instead of seven header buttons; Cost setup is a tab, not three separate links.\n• Settings group into sections with named switches and the property key as small print.\n• Off features say they are off, in words, with who can switch them on, instead of failing requests in the background.\n• Add people (new screen): several at once from the Microsoft directory, or by hand with email, name, department, team and project code; choose their role and give environment access in the same step. Today: one person at a time from a dialog. Department and project code are new fields on the user record; team uses the existing teams.\n\nEvery screen fits one 1440 × 900 window: no horizontal scrollbar, and no vertical one either. Long lists show a page of rows with page controls; extra detail sits in tabs or a side column."
  },
